##
    Asking for bits where there are none.

    This program does not compile. The operations that work on bits work on whole numbers and
    nothing else: a real or a fraction holds a value, and how that value is stored is the
    runtime's business rather than the program's.

    The interesting refusal is the first. In C# the same symbol covers exclusive-or on booleans
    and on bits alike, so reaching for 'xor' on two booleans is the mistake a C# reader makes —
    and '!=' already asks that question, which is what the message says.
##

shared model Program

    function Main()
        Program.Booleans();
        Program.NotWholeNumbers();
        Program.ShiftingTooFar();
        Program.TheWrongWord();
    end function

    # '!=' is the spelling this language keeps for the question.
    function Booleans()
        boolean ready = true;
        boolean willing = false;

        Console.WriteLine(ready xor willing);
    end function

    # A real and a fraction have no bits to speak of.
    function NotWholeNumbers()
        real measured = 1.5;
        fraction third = 1|3;

        Console.WriteLine(measured bitwise and 3);
        Console.WriteLine(third shiftleft 1);
    end function

    ##
        An integer holds 64 bits, so moving further than that leaves nothing behind. C# folds
        the amount into range and quietly shifts by none; here the amount is read as written.
    ##
    function ShiftingTooFar()
        Console.WriteLine(1 shiftleft 64);
        Console.WriteLine(1 shiftright -1);
    end function

    # Only 'and' and 'or' need qualifying, because only those two already mean something.
    function TheWrongWord()
        Console.WriteLine(6 bitwise not 3);
    end function

end model
